<Docs edit="site/src/pages/editor.astro" title="Editor" description="The web page every Facet panel serves: build your own pages card by card on a live copy of the panel, set the built-in screens and the panel up, save to the panel." group="Use Facet" current={url('/editor/')} headings={headings}>
  <p class="lede">
    Every Facet panel serves its own editor. Open the panel's address in a browser to build your own pages, card by
    card, on a live copy of the panel, then save them to it. Nothing to install: the page comes from the panel itself.
    This is the editor's first version: more is coming, and some of it will change.
  </p>

  <figure class="page-shot"><img src={url('/editor/editor.png')} width="2560" height="1570" alt="The editor: a live copy of the panel showing its Home, and the screen's name, order and start page beside it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The editor: the live panel in the middle, what you picked beside it</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="open">Opening it</h2>
  <p>
    On a computer or phone on the same Wi-Fi, open <code>http://</code> and the panel's address, for example
    <code>http://192.168.1.50/</code>. The address is on the panel: a fresh panel's empty Home shows it, and so does
    <strong>Settings &gt; About</strong>. The first time after an update the editor downloads the panel's renderer
    (about 1.5 MB, a few seconds) and shows how far it has got; after that it opens at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    On a phone held upright the whole panel stays in view and what you picked opens in a sheet from the bottom: drag
    it up for more room, and <strong>Done</strong>, its X or a tap on the panel closes it. Held sideways, it sits
    beside the panel as on a computer.
  </p>
  <p>
    The panel in the middle of the page is drawn by the panel's own renderer, with the panel's own data: your lights
    and sensors, the flights overhead, the weather, your calendar, the album art of what is playing (the panel
    passes the picture on from Home Assistant). What you see there is what the glass will show.
  </p>

  <h2 id="screens">Your screens</h2>
  <p>
    The tabs along the top are your own screens, in the order you swipe through them on the panel. A fresh panel has
    one, an empty Home. <strong>+ Screen</strong> adds another (up to eight), or puts a card for one of the built-in
    screens on the screen you are on; pick a tab to name it, move it earlier or later, choose it as the screen the panel
    opens on, or remove it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cards">Cards</h2>
  <h3 id="add">Adding a card</h3>
  <p>
    Tap an empty square on the live panel (or <strong>+ Add a card at the end</strong>). <strong>Size</strong> says
    whether the new card fills the square (<strong>1 × 1</strong>) or its top half (<strong>Half</strong>). Pick what
    it is first: a <strong>Screen</strong> (a card that opens one of your screens or a built-in one),
    <strong>Light</strong>, <strong>Switch</strong>, <strong>Media</strong>, <strong>Climate</strong>,
    <strong>Cover</strong>, <strong>Sensor</strong> or <strong>Other</strong>; on a half there is also
    <strong>State readout</strong>. A Sensor asks what it measures: temperature, humidity, power, energy, battery, doors
    and windows, or motion and presence. Then every entity of that type is listed, each with its value; type part of
    its name or entity id to narrow the list. Typing straight away, before picking a type, searches everything.
  </p>
  <div class="page-shots">
    <figure><img src={url('/editor/types.png')} width="840" height="1200" alt="Add a card: the size, a search box, and the card types under it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The card types</figcaption></figure>
    <figure><img src={url('/editor/search.png')} width="840" height="940" alt="Every temperature sensor, each with its name, entity id and temperature, and a box to filter them." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Every entity of the type, each with its value</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3 id="search">Searching your entities</h3>
  <p>
    Each entity shows its value the way Home Assistant does: <em>21.5 °C</em>, <em>Open</em>, <em>Detected</em>. When
    you start adding a card, the panel reads your entities from Home Assistant once (a few seconds in a large house) and
    hands the editor a compact index of them: each one's id, name, what it measures and its value. The lists and the
    search run on that index, so they are instant, even with thousands of sensors. The index is read again in the background when it is over a minute old.
    The panel keeps none of it once it has sent it, and only uses memory it can spare; if a very large house does not
    fit, the editor says how many entities it could not include.
  </p>
  <p>
    The search reads through the panel's own Home Assistant link (Settings &gt; Home Assistant). Without one, you can
    still add Screen cards.
  </p>

  <h3 id="sizes">Sizes and changing a card</h3>
  <p>Every card can be any size. The grid is three squares across on both panels.</p>
  <figure class="page-shot" style="max-width: 420px"><Shot id="home" alt="A Home with a 2 by 1 Weather card, a 1 by 1 Clock card, a 2 by 2 Media card, a 1 by 1 light card and two half sensor cards." /><figcaption>2 × 1 and 1 × 1, 2 × 2 and 1 × 1, then two halves</figcaption></figure>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>1 × 1</strong>: a tile, the name top-left and the icon top-right, the state at the bottom. A long name takes two lines.</li>
    <li><strong>Half</strong>: half a square, the name top-left and the icon top-right over the state. Two halves share one square: tap a half, then <strong>+ Add another half</strong> under it. The <strong>State readout</strong> is a half only: a sensor's state with no box and no tap, in the accent colour while it is on.</li>
    <li><strong>2 × 1</strong> and <strong>2 × 2</strong>: the card with its picture, such as the record for Media or the animated icon for Weather.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Tap a card on the live panel to change it: its size, its entity (type to search for another), its name (leave it
    empty to use the entity's own) and what a tap does (toggle it, open its screen, or nothing), or remove it. Cards fill
    the grid in reading order; the cards after a resized one move to fit. A card that opens a built-in screen also shows
    that screen's look, such as Media's visual or the Calendar's view, with a row to all its settings.
  </p>

  <h3 id="move">Moving a card</h3>
  <p>
    Drag a card on the live panel (on a touch screen, press and hold it first). A dashed outline shows where it will
    land, and the other cards move out of its way in order; let go to keep it there, or press Esc to put it back. With a
    keyboard, the arrow keys move the picked card one place; <strong>Earlier</strong> and <strong>Later</strong> do the
    same. One <strong>Undo</strong> puts a move back.
  </p>

  <h2 id="built-in">Built-in screens and Settings</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Settings</strong> opens the panel's settings as one list, as on the panel itself:
    <strong>Screens</strong> holds the built-in screens (Clock, Weather, Media, Calendar, Energy, Overhead and the rest),
    each with its name, where its data comes from and its options, and the live panel shows the one you pick. Every
    built-in screen is always on the panel; a card opens it, and it reads its data only while it shows or a card for it
    is on Home.
    <strong>Home Assistant</strong> sets the panel's own link (its address and a token). Then the panel's look, text
    size, units and the rest, each shown on the live panel as you change it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="save">Saving</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing reaches the panel until you press <strong>Save</strong>. It lists your changes in words, the panel checks
    them, and they go to the panel; if the panel refuses something, the editor says what and where.
    <strong>Undo</strong> puts the last save back. <strong>Back to factory</strong> starts again from one empty Home.
  </p>

  <h2 id="next">What is coming</h2>
  <p>
    This is the first version of the editor. Next: taps on the live panel that change a screen's own view (picking a
    plane on Overhead, opening a sheet) work there too, and more card layouts per size. Follow it on <a href="https://github.com/mrdarrengriffin/facet/issues/82">GitHub</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
